userStories.description:
**Goal** — Read owned index/item threads and distinguish settled items from items reopened by writer replies.

**Motivation** — Those rulings live in a git-ignored sidecar (`src/workflow/decisions-sidecar.ts`) next to the run, so a fresh checkout (a `nax bot` checkout, a koda job) cannot see them, and nobody can rule from the PR.

**Approach** — "Own" comment everywhere in this spec has `ownComments` semantics (`src/forge/marker.ts`): authored by the current forge login AND carrying a valid `nax-bot:v1` marker. A human reply posted under the same login as nax bot (today's default: nax posts as the user's own `gh`/`glab` login) has no marker, so it is not own, and it is a reply like any other.

`src/workflow/item-thread-state.ts` — `readItemThreads(threads, comments, login)`, `settleItemThreads(read, canRule)`. `canRule = write access of a reply's author. settled = has a ruling and no kept reply after it.` Only threads whose key is in the index are considered; a thread outside the index is ignored. The ruling comes from the last own comment when its key starts with `wr-`; replies after the last own comment that are not own are candidates, and only accepted writer replies enter kept. A canRule failure means no access for that reply: it creates no ruling and does not invalidate an existing echo, so an item with an existing ruling and no accepted later reply remains settled.

**Scope** — In: shared reading/settling state for publish, record and promote. Out: actual forge permission lookup (US-001/009), keyword/model interpretation (US-003/010), posting threads (US-005/006). Resolving an item thread without replying does not rule.

**Interface**
```typescript
export interface ItemThread {
  readonly key: string; readonly itemId: string; readonly kind: string; readonly thread: PrThread;
  readonly ruling?: PrRuling;               // from the last own echo, if any
  readonly replies: readonly PrComment[];   // comments after the last own comment that are not own
}
export interface ReadItemThreads {
  readonly index?: ItemIndex;               // from the own `w-index` comment
  readonly indexComment?: PrComment;
  readonly items: readonly ItemThread[];    // own threads whose opener key starts with "w-"
}
export interface SettledItems {
  readonly settled: readonly (ItemThread & { readonly ruling: PrRuling })[];
  readonly open: readonly (ItemThread & { readonly kept: readonly PrComment[] })[];
}
/** canRule = write access of a reply's author. settled = has a ruling and no kept reply after it. */
export function settleItemThreads(read: ReadItemThreads, canRule: (c: PrComment) => Promise<boolean>): Promise<SettledItems>;
```

**Goal** — Add an isolated fast-tier complete operation and protected prompt for interpreting collaborator replies as candidate rulings.

**Motivation** — Those rulings live in a git-ignored sidecar (`src/workflow/decisions-sidecar.ts`) next to the run, so a fresh checkout (a `nax bot` checkout, a koda job) cannot see them, and nobody can rule from the PR.

**Approach** — `BotConfig.models` (`src/config/schemas-bot.ts`). Target: adds `rulings: ConfiguredModelSchema.optional()`; `botModel` role union gains `"rulings"`, handled by its own branch that resolves `override ?? config.bot?.models.rulings ?? "fast"` (no workflow or finish fallback).

`src/prompts/builders/bot-ruling-builder.ts` — `buildBotRulingReadPrompt(input)`. `src/operations/bot-ruling-read.ts` — `botRulingReadOp`, with its own types (no import from `src/workflow`). `botChecksExtractOp` — the precedent for a fast-tier `complete` op that parses with `tryParseLLMJson` (`src/operations/bot-checks-extract.ts`).

Put each item's id, kind and issue and its reply's id, author and body under an untrusted-data heading inside fences the data cannot terminate. Request one JSON object with rulings, allowed dispositions fixed/waived/confirmed/overridden/verified/none, a one-line reason, supporting quote copied verbatim and optional fixed SHA; ambiguous meaning must return none. Parse fenced JSON with tryParseLLMJson; ignore unknown input ids and invalid dispositions, return null for no JSON object. The successful parse result is the BotRulingReadVerdict object containing its rulings array, matching the complete operation's declared output and the readModel callback, not a bare array. Add the config key description in src/cli/config-descriptions.ts and exports in @/prompts and @/operations. Use normal operation dispatch, not a direct nax-ai call.

**Scope** — In: schema/model resolution, prompt, complete op/types/parser and tests. Out: permission checks and persisted echoes (US-009), quote/SHA acceptance and batch/ask orchestration (US-010), command wiring (US-011/012). A nax bot ruling other than `fixed` is never created by any path; this op interprets a human reply and creates no ruling itself.

**Interface**
```typescript
export type RulingDisposition = "fixed" | "waived" | "confirmed" | "overridden" | "verified" | "none";
export interface BotRulingReadInput {
  readonly items: readonly { readonly id: string; readonly kind: string; readonly issue: string;
    readonly reply: { readonly id: string; readonly author: string; readonly body: string } }[];
  readonly model?: ConfiguredModel;
}
export interface BotRulingReadVerdict {
  readonly rulings: readonly { readonly id: string; readonly disposition: RulingDisposition;
    readonly reason: string; readonly quote: string; readonly sha?: string }[];
}
```
`botRulingReadOp` has kind `complete` and model from `input.model`; `buildBotRulingReadPrompt(input)` is exported from @/prompts.

**Goal** — Record authorized keyword rulings as owned thread echoes, resolve settled threads and tick matching body checklist lines.

**Motivation** — Those rulings live in a git-ignored sidecar (`src/workflow/decisions-sidecar.ts`) next to the run, so a fresh checkout (a `nax bot` checkout, a koda job) cannot see them, and nobody can rule from the PR.

**Approach** — `recordPrRulings(input)` order:

1. `read = readItemThreads(threads, comments, login)`; `canRule(c)` = `hasWriteAccess(ref, { login: c.author, id: c.authorId }, deps, cwd)`, memoised per login; a throw counts as no access and is logged.
2. `{ settled, open } = settleItemThreads(read, canRule)`. Only threads whose key is in the index are considered; a thread outside the index is ignored.
3. For each open item with kept replies, only the newest kept reply is read: `parseRulingKeyword(body)`; a ruling is that item's candidate (`by` = the reply's author).
5. Each candidate ruling: `replyToThread` with the echo, then `resolveThread`. A settled item whose thread is unresolved gets `resolveThread` again (no new echo).
6. The PR body is re-read with `readPrInfo` and ticked with `tickChecklist` of one `DecisionEntry` per settled item whose id is on a checklist line (kind mapped as in Integration, `sha` for `fixed`), written with `editBodyArgv` only when it changed.

`recordPrRulings` never creates a bot ruling; only `postEarlier` does (verdict `fixed`). The sidecar kind of an item is its `kind`, except `ruling`, which maps to `advisor` (the mapping `sidecarKindFor` applies to an `OpenItem`).

Failure behavior: `hasWriteAccess` throws for a replier — treated as no write access, logged. Echo reply fails — ruling not recorded (item open), counted in `failed`. `resolveThread` fails after an echo — logged; the item is settled; resolved again next call. Body edit fails — logged; readiness does not read ticks. Preserve the index in the outcome, and count only successful echoes as newly recorded.

**Scope** — In: src/bot/rulings.ts recording steps 1-3, 5-6, permission memoization, keyword candidates, echo/resolve/body ticking and tests. Out: model batching/validation/asks (US-010), complete operation (US-008), command wiring (US-011/012), promotion merge (US-016). The `readModel` input is accepted but not yet called. US-008 is not a dependency of this story: keep the intermediate callback structurally typed without requiring a runtime import from a future file, then use the operation types at US-010 integration. Replies from non-writers must not be answered or reopen an item.

**Interface**
```typescript
export interface RecordRulingsInput {
  readonly ref: PrRef; readonly info: PrInfo; readonly login: string;
  readonly threads: readonly PrThread[]; readonly comments: readonly PrComment[];
  readonly deps: ForgeDeps; readonly cwd: string;
  readonly readModel: (input: BotRulingReadInput) => Promise<BotRulingReadVerdict | null>;
}
export interface RecordRulingsOutcome {
  readonly index?: ItemIndex;
  readonly recorded: readonly PrRuling[];
  readonly settled: SettledItems["settled"];
  readonly open: SettledItems["open"];
  readonly asked: number;
  readonly failed: number;
}
```
The final callback's structural input contains `items` with id/kind/issue and reply id/author/body plus optional model; its output contains `rulings` with id/disposition/reason/quote/optional sha. US-010 supplies its operation-owned named types. Use forge dependency seams for writes and logged failures, with no sidecar file writes.

The named types `BotRulingReadInput` and `BotRulingReadVerdict` are imported with `import type` only (or declared as an inline structural equivalent) until US-010 wires the model call.
